      "writtenOn": "2022-11-21T16:56:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Q: This logic feels a bit strange:\nIf the driver doesn\u0027t provide a resume callback \u003d\u003e dev-\u003epowered \u003d 1 (So I imagine it is always powered on)\nIf the driver doesn\u0027t provide a suspend callback \u003d\u003e dev-\u003epowered \u003d 0\nWhy don\u0027t we modify the state of dev-\u003epowered in the suspend/resume callbacks ?",
      "revId": "f5b7aaefd01c4cb7854c4cd5b79eb65c6e921bed",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b36eebbb_fb0e0431",
        "filename": "arch/arm64/kvm/hyp/nvhe/iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 318,
      "author": {
        "id": 1059815
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-11-21T17:25:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The host is the one in charge of power here. The callbacks are just for the driver to react to power changes. Maybe the callbacks should be called `on_resume` and `on_suspend` to make it clearer?\n\nAnother thing to note is that the `dev-\u003epowered` is really just a boolean to identify which devices should be updated when a page changes ownership. So `resume` is an opportunity for the driver to return zero if the device was found ready or non-zero otherwise. In the latter case EL2 will ignore a powered IOMMU.",
      "parentUuid": "0aab3b14_6ca476e0",
      "revId": "f5b7aaefd01c4cb7854c4cd5b79eb65c6e921bed",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
